(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ocular-trauma-quiz"]={"title":"Ocular Trauma Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What distinguishes an orbital contusion from a periorbital haematoma?","choices":["Contusion follows sharp trauma; the haematoma follows blunt","Contusion needs surgery; the haematoma does not","Contusion has no haemorrhage; the haematoma bleeds in the orbit","Contusion is bilateral; the haematoma is unilateral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The septum is what keeps the contusion in front of the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A child with a quiet-looking eye vomits and becomes bradycardic each time he looks up after facial trauma. What does that combination represent?","choices":["Concussion alone","Muscle entrapment","Raised intracranial pressure","Vasovagal response to pain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe pain, bradycardia and vomiting on eye movement point to entrapment. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Ocular trauma is the leading cause of monocular blindness in which group?","choices":["Elderly women","Children under five","Adolescent girls","Young adult men"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is the group in which it leads. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"An open globe injury is suspected. What is placed over the eye?","choices":["A pressure patch","A saline-soaked gauze dressing","Nothing, so the eye can be re-examined","A rigid protective shield"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It keeps pressure off the globe until repair. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Clear fluid drips from a patient's nose after head trauma and leaves a ring within a ring on the bedsheet. What is this?","choices":["A cerebrospinal fluid leak","Simple epistaxis","Lacrimal duct injury","Cerebral oedema"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inner ring of blood, outer ring of cerebrospinal fluid. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Why may a facial laceration be left open for 24 hours before closure?","choices":["It reduces the need for antibiotics","It allows swelling to resolve first","It is required by tetanus prophylaxis","The face is highly vascular"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That vascularity is what permits the delay. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A traction retinal detachment is most commonly caused by what?","choices":["Blunt ocular trauma alone","Proliferative diabetic retinopathy","Age-related macular degeneration","Uveal melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrovascular tissue pulls the retina off. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why is oral aminocaproic acid used in hyphema?","choices":["It prevents secondary infection","It dilates the pupil to rest the ciliary body","It slows clot breakdown","It lowers the intraocular pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an antifibrinolytic. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Over what period does most rebleeding after hyphema occur?","choices":["The first 24 hours only","After the first month","Rebleeding is not a recognised complication","The first 72 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is when a secondary haemorrhage is most likely. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A metal fragment may be lodged in the eye after grinding. Which study is ordered?","choices":["Computed tomography of the orbit","Magnetic resonance imaging of the orbit","Ocular ultrasound with pressure on the globe","No imaging unless vision is reduced"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It answers the question without moving the fragment. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A patient has numbness of the cheek after an orbital fracture. Which nerve is injured?","choices":["The trochlear nerve","The infraorbital nerve","The optic nerve","The facial nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infraorbital numbness is a recognised finding. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which instruction must a patient with a corneal abrasion never be given?","choices":["Avoid rubbing the eye","Take home topical anaesthetic drops for pain","Use the topical antibiotic as prescribed","Return if symptoms worsen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They delay healing, mask worsening symptoms and can cause a corneal ulcer. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which three factors bring a rhegmatogenous detachment forward?","choices":["Diabetes, hypertension and smoking","Contact lens wear, allergy and blepharitis","Sickle cell disease, burns and transfusion","Myopia, cataract surgery and ocular trauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is usually age-related, but these accelerate it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why are antibiotics for a cerebrospinal fluid leak controversial?","choices":["They risk selecting resistant organisms","They interfere with computed tomography","They are contraindicated with a skull fracture","They mask the halo sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is the stated concern. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A patient has diplopia looking UP after a blow to the orbit. Which muscle is entrapped?","choices":["Levator palpebrae","Inferior rectus","Medial rectus","Superior oblique"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Upward gaze diplopia means the inferior rectus or its supporting structures. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"At which sites does a globe most often rupture under blunt force?","choices":["The limbus alone","The macula and optic disc","Posterior to the muscle insertions, old incisions, the lamina cribrosa","The central cornea and the visual axis, where the wall is thinnest"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those are the weak points. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Why is ophthalmology consulted for a true blowout fracture even without entrapment?","choices":["About 30% sustain a significant globe injury","Because every fracture needs operative repair","Because the optic nerve is always damaged","To exclude a basilar skull fracture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The globe has to be assessed. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which set of symptoms fits a retinal detachment?","choices":["Pain on eye movement with loss of colour vision","Bilateral disc swelling with transient obscurations","A curtain, floaters, flashes and a field defect","Gradual painless loss of the peripheral field over years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cloudy or smoky vision belongs there too. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Why is a basilar skull fracture often identified from indirect signs?","choices":["It often produces no symptoms of its own","The fracture is always visible on plain films","It never causes bleeding","Patients are always unconscious"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The indirect signs are what give it away. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What proportion of full-thickness lid lacerations come with a corneal laceration or globe rupture?","choices":["About one in twenty","About one in ten","Almost none","About two thirds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Always look underneath a full-thickness lid cut. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A laceration sits 5 millimetres from the medial canthus. Why does that location matter?","choices":["It always indicates a globe rupture","It cannot be closed surgically","The canalicular system may be transected","The optic nerve runs immediately beneath"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If it is not repaired properly the tearing is permanent. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A patient struck in the eye has blood layered in the anterior chamber. What is this, and what else must be considered?","choices":["Hyphema, which can itself be a sign of open globe","Subconjunctival haemorrhage over the iris","Vitreous haemorrhage seen through the pupil","Corneal blood staining"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is not always an isolated injury. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"When does a corneal foreign body need ophthalmology rather than removal at the bedside?","choices":["When the patient wears contact lenses","When there is a rust ring","When it has been present more than a day","When it may have passed through the cornea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That is an open globe, not a surface foreign body. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A child has severe pain, bradycardia and vomiting on attempting to look up, but the eye looks quiet. What is this?","choices":["Concussion without orbital injury","A white-eyed blowout","Simple periorbital contusion","A retrobulbar haemorrhage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In children entrapment may come with no orbital soft tissue signs at all. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which examination shows the extent of a corneal abrasion?","choices":["Slit lamp fluorescein","Tonometry","Gonioscopy","Dilated fundoscopy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluorescein stains the exposed basement membrane. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A rust ring remains after a metallic foreign body is removed. How is it dealt with?","choices":["A burr tip drill","Repeated irrigation with saline","A cotton-tipped applicator","Leaving it to resorb"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That is how a rust ring comes out. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which history point matters because surgery may follow?","choices":["Whether the patient wears contact lenses at night","Family history of glaucoma","When the patient last ate and drank","The patient's usual spectacle prescription"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nil-by-mouth status shapes the anaesthetic plan. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which set of measures accompanies the shield in a suspected open globe?","choices":["Topical anaesthetic drops to take home","Antiemetics, analgesia, tetanus and computed tomography","A mydriatic, tonometry and dilated fundoscopy of the retina","Copious irrigation and a pressure patch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the patient from straining protects the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A partial-thickness lid laceration meets none of the referral criteria. How is it handled?","choices":["Closed with tissue adhesive and no follow-up","Referred to plastic surgery routinely","Repair there, ophthalmology in 2 to 3 days","Immediate transfer for operative repair"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It does not need the specialist to close it. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Within what period must a patient with a retinal detachment see ophthalmology?","choices":["Only if the vision worsens","Within 24 hours","Within one week","At the next routine clinic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The referral is immediate. — CMS I Ocular Trauma - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
